1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:14,184
A homeowner sent the bank holding her mortgage a bill of exchange for
$244,663.79, drawn, she said, on a trust account run through the IRS.

2
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,496
She argued that once the bank
received it, "this loan is paid off".

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,944
Is there really a secret Treasury account
in your name that can pay off your debts?

4
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:34,015
The story behind her bill points at real things, your birth certificate, the
Treasury, the commercial code, so every step sounds like something you could check.

5
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:44,977
It starts with your name in capital letters. The site jonhoyle.com says "The all capitals
spelling of the name creates a corporate fiction", which believers call the strawman.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,456
Next comes your birth certificate, which the site Understand Contract Law treats as
an investment: "These birth certificate bonds have a value and are being traded".

7
00:00:54,467 --> 00:01:05,171
And the value is supposed to sit in an account you can reach: jonhoyle.com says "there is an
alphanumeric account number in your Strawman name that is your private account that can be drawn from".

8
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:15,664
The way in is a stamp: in one YouTube how-to video, a red stamp with your Social Security
number on a bill is "what's going to give you access into your treasury account".

9
00:01:15,667 --> 00:01:27,355
The kit is for sale: the Treasury notes that some websites sell videos, webinars and coaching on
how to do this, and that "No one has profited from the Treasury Department by using these tactics".

10
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:31,559
So start with the account
itself. Does it exist?

11
00:01:31,567 --> 00:01:36,895
The Treasury says these accounts "are fictitious
and do not exist in the Treasury system".

12
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:49,260
A federal appeals court, the Sixth Circuit, said the same: the Treasury "does not maintain depository
accounts against which an individual can draw a check, draft, or any other financial instrument".

13
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:56,887
The IRS puts it more bluntly: "The notion of secret
accounts assigned to each citizen is pure fantasy".

14
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:01,236
What about the birth certificate
that is meant to fill it?

15
00:02:01,267 --> 00:02:08,707
The Treasury's answer: "There is no monetary value to a birth
certificate", and a birth certificate cannot be used to buy anything.

16
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:26,037
In Gravatt v. United States, a man told the Court of Federal Claims that his birth certificate was a contract with the United States, and
the court called that frivolous: birth certificates, it said, do not "recognize or impose contractual rights, obligations, or duties".

17
00:02:26,067 --> 00:02:37,491
He also claimed to be owed money from a trust fund held in the Treasury, and the court wrote that it
"strains all logic and reason to believe that the trust fund alleged by plaintiff actually exists".

18
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:53,340
And the strawman itself? A history of these movements in Canada says it "has no basis in law in either the US or Canada", and
in Australia the Supreme Court of Queensland found that the idea "does not find any reflection in any provision of our law".

19
00:02:54,367 --> 00:02:56,239
That leaves the stamped bill.

20
00:02:56,267 --> 00:03:06,443
The Treasury says "All these Bills of Exchange drawn on the U.S. Treasury are worthless",
and that drawing such drafts on it "is fraudulent and a violation of federal law".

21
00:03:06,467 --> 00:03:22,499
In Canada, a family whose wood lots were financed by Farm Credit Canada said they had paid with bonds drawn on secret accounts run
by the US Treasury; the New Brunswick Court of Appeal found the strategy frivolous and illogical, and said the family knew this.

22
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:29,676
As for the homeowner, the bank told her the bill was an
unacceptable form of payment, and went on to foreclose on her home.

23
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,320
When she sued, the federal judge found that she "did not tender payment, but rather a
worthless piece of paper", while accepting that her belief in the process seemed genuine.

24
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:52,373
The man whose redemption advice she had followed, Barton Buhtz, had just been convicted
in Oregon, with several co-defendants, of fraud charges for passing bills of exchange.

25
00:03:52,700 --> 00:04:07,532
Sending one can be a crime by itself: in United States v. Heath, the Sixth Circuit upheld a conviction for presenting
a fictitious financial instrument, a "Registered Bill of Exchange" sent to the IRS with no actual account behind it.

26
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:16,989
How often an ordinary user ends up charged, none of these sources counts; what
they record are the convictions of a promoter and of people who passed the bills.

27
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:27,200
So the answer to the first question is no: the Treasury says the account does not exist,
and the courts in this evidence that looked at these bills treated them as worthless.

28
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:37,368
With the account gone, the promoters fall back on the idea underneath it:
that ever since the country left gold, no debt can truly be paid at all.

29
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:46,520
The site Educated in Law states it in one line: "As a result
of HJR 192, the people can no longer pay their debts."

30
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:56,529
A video called Take Back Your Strawman Birth Certificate Bonds says the change
"eliminated our ability to pay our debts allowing only for their discharge".

31
00:04:56,533 --> 00:04:59,173
So what does that resolution actually say?

32
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:09,540
The judge in that mortgage case read it, and it is a real law from 1933, headed
"To assure uniform value to the coins and currencies of the United States".

33
00:05:09,867 --> 00:05:18,387
It made contract clauses demanding payment in gold against public
policy, and it declared US currency "legal tender for all debts".

34
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:31,384
A 1939 ruling the homeowner also cited, Guaranty Trust Co. v. Henwood, explained what Congress
wanted: a debt in dollars is measured by the "actual number of dollars promised, dollar for dollar".

35
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:41,984
Nothing in it mentions secret trust accounts or bills of exchange, the judge wrote;
it addresses "nothing more than the U.S. monetary shift away from the gold standard".

36
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,728
The notes in your wallet count too: as one federal appeals court
put it, "Congress has declared federal reserve notes legal tender".

37
00:05:51,033 --> 00:06:02,097
In a tax case, the Ninth Circuit rejected as "frivolous" the argument that Federal
Reserve Notes "are not valid currency, cannot be taxed, and are merely" debts.

38
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:09,468
So the answer to the second question is no: leaving gold
changed what backs a dollar, not whether a dollar pays a debt.

39
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:21,564
Both answers meet in the same place: the Treasury says the scheme's story
starts with 1933, and the 1933 law says the opposite of what that story needs.

40
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:29,199
So if a way to pay needs you to believe the Treasury is hiding
your money, expect your lender to treat it as no payment at all.

41
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:35,284
And before you stamp anything, ask whoever is
selling the method for one court that accepted it.
